# Carol

## Act 1

Written 2026-11-23 03:18.

### Who I am

I am Carol. When I look at a figure I ask what I may do to it, and what is
still true afterwards. Whatever is still true is what the figure was about.

### First: can topology alone prove it?

No. And the reason is short enough to give in full.

Topology allows any bending and stretching that does not tear and does not
glue. Under such a change every triangle can be turned into every other
triangle. A right triangle with the sides 3, 4 and 5 becomes a triangle
with the sides 3, 4 and 6, and that one has no right angle.

So a statement that topology can make is true of both triangles or false of
both. The theorem is true of the first and false of the second, since
9 + 16 is 25 and not 36. Topology cannot tell them apart. It cannot state
the theorem, and what cannot be stated cannot be proved.

This is not a gap I could close with more work. "Right angle" and "length"
are among the things bending destroys.

So I asked how much less I may do to a figure before the theorem becomes
sayable.

| What I allow | What survives | Can the theorem be said? |
|---|---|---|
| bending and stretching | being in one piece, having three corners | no |
| shearing, and stretching in one direction | straight lines, parallels, ratios of areas | no |
| scaling, the same in every direction | angles, ratios of lengths, shape | **yes** |
| moving and turning only | all of the above, and lengths themselves | yes |

Shearing fails for the same reason as bending. A shear turns a right
triangle into one that is not right. With shears, one-way stretches and
moves together, any triangle can be turned into any other.

Scaling is where it first holds. Scale a right triangle by a factor k. The
angle stays right. The sides become ka, kb and kc. Both sides of the
equation are multiplied by k². So the equation is true of the scaled
triangle exactly when it was true before.

That is what I can honestly say. The theorem is not about size. It is about
shape. It needs lengths only as ratios.

**The nearest honest thing, then:** a proof by similarity. A right triangle
contains two smaller copies of itself, scaled. What scaling leaves alone,
the ratio of one side to another, is the same in all three. I will prove
the theorem from that and from nothing about areas, coordinates or motion.

I will not call it a topological proof. It is not one.

### The theorem

In a flat plane, let ABC be a triangle whose angle at C is a right angle.
Let a be the length of the side BC, b the length of the side CA, and c the
length of the side AB, all measured in one unit. The side AB lies opposite
the right angle. Then

```
a · a + b · b = c · c
```

Three things about what this says.

- a, b and c are numbers. a² is the number a times itself. It is not a
  square drawn on the side. That reading is a second statement. It becomes
  the same one only after somebody grants that a square of side a has the
  area a².
- It is about a flat plane. On a globe it is false.
- It says nothing in the other direction. That a triangle with
  a² + b² = c² must have a right angle is the converse. It is true, and it
  is another theorem.

### What I take for granted

Definitions.

1. **Definition.** Point, straight line, and segment: the part of a line
   between two points, with those two points.
2. **Definition.** The length of a segment is a positive number, once a
   unit is chosen.
3. **Definition.** A point H is between A and B when it lies on the
   segment AB and is neither A nor B.
4. **Definition.** An angle is formed by two rays from one point. A
   straight angle is formed by the two halves of a line. A right angle is
   half of a straight angle. An acute angle is smaller than a right angle.
5. **Definition.** A triangle is three points not on one line, with the
   three segments joining them. It has three sides and three angles.
6. **Definition.** A right triangle is a triangle with one right angle.
   The side opposite the right angle is the hypotenuse.
7. **Definition.** Two lines are perpendicular when they meet at a right
   angle. The foot of the perpendicular from a point to a line is the
   point where the perpendicular through that point meets the line.
8. **Definition.** Two triangles are similar when their corners can be
   matched so that matched angles are equal and all three pairs of matched
   sides have one and the same ratio.

Axioms.

9. **Axiom.** Lengths obey the ordinary rules of arithmetic. In
   particular x(y + z) = xy + xz, and an equation may be multiplied on both
   sides by the same number.
10. **Axiom.** Lengths add along a line: if H is between A and B, then
    AH + HB = AB.
11. **Axiom.** The parallel axiom: through a point not on a line there is
    exactly one line that does not meet it. I do not use it directly. I
    list it because items 12 and 13 are false without it, and I want that
    seen.

Known results.

12. **Known result.** The three angles of a triangle together make a
    straight angle.
13. **Known result.** If two angles of one triangle equal two angles of
    another, the triangles are similar, with the equal angles matched.
14. **Known result.** From a point not on a line there is exactly one
    perpendicular to that line.
15. **Known result.** When a ray stands on a line, the two angles it makes
    with the line together make a straight angle.

That is the whole list. I use no area, no coordinates, no congruence, and
I move nothing.

One thing is not on the list because I will prove it and not assume it:
that the foot of the perpendicular from C falls between A and B. Most
drawings simply show it there.

### What I would not grant without argument

**To Dave.** That the pieces fit. When a figure is cut and the parts are
moved, three things are being assumed, and each one needs its reason:

- that edges which look like one straight line are one, which is a
  statement about angles adding to a straight angle;
- that the figure left in the middle is a square, which needs four right
  angles and not only four equal sides;
- that area exists at all, that it does not change when a piece is moved,
  and that the area of the whole is the sum of the areas of its parts.

The last is three axioms. I do not object to them. I ask that they be on
the list.

**To Bob.** That the distance between two points is
√((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²). If that is his definition of length, the
theorem is inside the definition, and the algebra afterwards proves that he
can do algebra. I would ask him what a right angle is in his plane, what
length is, and why his plane of number pairs is the plane the rest of us
are drawing in.

**To everyone, myself first.** The flatness. My item 13 says that a small
triangle can have the shape of a large one. On a globe it cannot: there the
angles of a triangle fix its size. So scaling exists only in a flat plane,
and my whole proof stands on item 11. Anyone whose proof seems not to need
the parallel axiom has used it somewhere without noticing. I would like to
know where.

**To Percy.** Nothing to grant, since he assumes nothing. One request: a
triangle on a globe is not a counterexample. The theorem says "flat".

## Act 2

Written 2026-11-23 03:28. I have not read the others' Act 2.

### The idea

Drop the perpendicular from the right angle onto the long side. It cuts the
triangle into two smaller ones of the same shape as the whole, and because
shape fixes the ratios of the sides, each short side squared equals the long
side times the piece of it lying next to that short side. The two pieces
make up the long side, and that is the theorem.

### The steps

The triangle is ABC with the right angle at C. I write α for its angle at
A and β for its angle at B. The sides are a = BC, b = CA, c = AB.

```
            C
           /|\
        b / | \ a
         /  |  \
        A---H---B
            c
```

The picture is beside the steps. No step rests on it. Step 6 exists
because the picture is not allowed to say where H is.

Three items were missing from my list. They are 16, 17 and 18, given under
"Not used" and marked new. I cite them here by number.

1. α + β + the angle at C make a straight angle. *Item 12.*
2. The angle at C is half a straight angle. *It is right, by what is given
   and item 6; a right angle is half a straight angle, item 4.*
3. α + β is a right angle. *Steps 1 and 2, and item 16.*
4. α is acute, and so is β. *β is more than nothing, item 16, so α is a
   right angle less something, by step 3. The same for β.*
5. C does not lie on the line AB. *Item 5.*
6. There is exactly one perpendicular from C to the line AB. Call its foot
   H. H is on the line AB, and the lines CH and AB meet at right angles.
   *Step 5, item 14, item 7.*
7. H is not A, and H is not B. *If H were A, the line CA would be the
   perpendicular, and the angle at A would be right, item 7. By step 4 it
   is acute. The same for B.*
8. A is not between H and B. *Suppose it were. Then the rays from A
   through H and from A through B are the two halves of the line, item 18.
   The ray from A through C stands on that line, so the angle between AC
   and AH, together with α, makes a straight angle, item 15. That angle is
   therefore a straight angle less α, which by step 4 is more than a right
   angle. Now C, H and A are not on one line, by steps 5 and 7, so they
   form a triangle, item 5. Its angle at H is right, step 6. Its angle at A
   is more than right. Its angle at C is more than nothing, item 16.
   Together they are more than a straight angle. That contradicts
   item 12.*
9. B is not between H and A. *The argument of step 8, with A and B
   exchanged and β in place of α.*
10. H is between A and B. *H, A and B are three different points of one
    line, by steps 6 and 7. Exactly one is between the other two, item 17.
    It is not A, step 8, and not B, step 9. Item 3.*
11. In the triangle ACH, the angle at A is α. *H and B lie on the same
    side of A, by step 10 and item 17, so the ray from A through H is the
    ray from A through B, item 18. The angle is formed by the same two rays
    as α, item 4.*
12. In the triangle ACH, the angle at H is right. *Step 6.*
13. The triangles ACH and ABC are similar, with A matched to A, H to C,
    and C to B. *Two angles of the one equal two of the other: α and α by
    step 11, right and right by steps 12 and 2. Item 13.*
14. AH / b = b / c. *In step 13 the side AH is matched to AC, and the
    side AC to AB. Matched sides have one ratio, item 8. AC is b and AB is
    c. The lengths are not zero, item 2.*
15. b · b = c · AH. *Step 14, both sides multiplied by b · c. Item 9.*
16. In the triangle CBH, the angle at B is β and the angle at H is right.
    *As steps 11 and 12, with B in place of A.*
17. The triangles CBH and ABC are similar, with B matched to B, H to C,
    and C to A. *Step 16 and step 2. Item 13.*
18. BH / a = a / c. *In step 17 the side BH is matched to BC, and the
    side BC to BA. Item 8, item 2.*
19. a · a = c · BH. *Step 18, both sides multiplied by a · c. Item 9.*
20. AH + HB = c. *Step 10 and item 10.*
21. a · a + b · b = c · BH + c · AH = c · (BH + AH) = c · c. *Steps 19
    and 15, then item 9, then step 20.*

That is the theorem.

### Where the right angle is used

In three places, and the third is the one that matters.

- **Step 4.** The other two angles are acute. Without it step 10 fails,
  and H may fall outside AB.
- **Steps 13 and 17.** The right angle at H is matched to the right angle
  at C. This is the whole proof. The cut makes a right angle, and the
  triangle has one to match it.
- **Step 12 with step 16.** One cut serves both small triangles. The
  perpendicular makes a right angle on each side of H. A right angle is
  the one angle that is equal to what it leaves of a straight angle.

**For 60° at C.** Step 13 fails. The triangle ACH has the angles α, 90°
and 90° − α. The triangle ABC has α, 60° and 120° − α. No matching makes
two of them equal, except when β is itself a right angle, and that is a
different right triangle.

One can try to repair it by cutting at 60° and not at 90°. Then steps 13
to 19 go through, each with its own cut. But two cuts are needed, one for
each side, and they do not land on the same point. Take the triangle with
all sides 1 and all angles 60°. The cut that serves A lands on B, so the
piece is the whole side, 1. The cut that serves B lands on A, and its piece
is 1 as well. The pieces together are 2, and the side is 1. Step 20 fails,
and what comes out is

```
a · a + b · b = c · 2 = 2,   and   c · c = 1.
```

So at 60° the two copies overlap. At a right angle, and only there, they
meet exactly.

### Where flatness is used

| Step | Item | For what |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 12 | the angles of ABC make a straight angle |
| 8 and 9 | 12 | the foot cannot fall outside, or a triangle would have too much angle |
| 13 and 17 | 13 | a smaller triangle has the shape of a larger one |

Items 12 and 13 are both false on a ball. Item 13 is the deeper use. On a
ball a triangle's angles fix its size, so there is no smaller copy at all,
and steps 13 and 17 have nothing to say.

No step cites item 11, the parallel axiom. It stands behind items 12 and
13 and nowhere else.

### Used

2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14, 15, and the new 16, 17, 18.

### Not used

- **Item 1.** Point, line and segment. No step cites it. It is the
  vocabulary every step is written in.
- **Item 11.** The parallel axiom. Not cited, as said above. It is needed
  by whoever proves items 12 and 13.

My list in Act 1 was not complete. I said it was. These three were
missing, and the proof does not stand without them.

16. **Axiom, new.** Angles have sizes, and the sizes are added, taken away
    and compared as numbers are. Every angle of a triangle is more than
    nothing. *Used in steps 3, 4 and 8.*
17. **Axiom, new.** Of three different points on a line, exactly one is
    between the other two. *Used in steps 10 and 11.*
18. **Definition, new.** A point A on a line divides the rest of the line
    into two sides. Two points are on the same side when A is not between
    them. The ray from A through B is A together with every point on B's
    side. So two points on the same side of A give the same ray, and two
    points on opposite sides give the two halves of the line. *Used in
    steps 8 and 11.*

All three are about order and about angle as a quantity. I left them out
because a picture supplies them without being asked. That is the mistake I
warned Dave about in Act 1.

### The one thing my proof needs that I expect the others can do without

A triangle of one shape at two different sizes: item 13, which lets me
compare a small triangle with a large one, and which I expect neither a
proof by area nor a proof by cutting ever needs.
